{0 Rocq Frontend Boundary}

The current trusted implementation boundary is the elaborated Kairos core
language, not the extended surface language.

{[
Kx_surface_syntax.source
  -- Kx_elaborate.elaborate_source -->
Kx_ast.program
  -- Kairos_to_model -->
Verification_model.node_model
  -- verification / Why3 backend -->
proof obligations
]}

The existing Rocq development should be compared against the semantics of
[Kx_ast] / [Verification_model] and the obligation generation that follows.
Surface constructs such as finite-domain quantifiers, named actions, topology
blocks and LTL helpers are frontend elaborations.

{1 What This Means}

For the current implementation:

- local predicates are not a proof-kernel feature; they are expanded before the
  core pipeline;
- topology blocks are not a new temporal logic; they generate ordinary safety
  LTL formulas using [G], [X] and [W];
- [maintainedUntil] and [whileRouteLocked] are surface constructors for formulas
  already expressible in the existing LTL fragment;
- pure functions and enum literals are core constructs and must be accounted
  for when lowering to Why3 or to any formal semantic model.

{1 Future Formalization Obligation}

To connect the extended language to Rocq cleanly, add a separate elaboration
correctness theorem:

{[
surface_program P
  elaborates_to C
  sem_surface P traces
  <-> sem_core C traces
]}

The current codebase does not yet formalize this theorem. Until it does, Rocq
should treat the elaborated AST as the formal entry point and document the
surface elaborator as an implementation component whose behavior is tested.
